# Rust Coding Rules for ZenLavaTerm

## 1. Rust Edition & Idioms
- Target Rust 2021 edition (`rustc 1.85+`).
- Write idiomatic, readable, high-performance Rust.
- Keep dependencies minimal. Before adding any new crate to `Cargo.toml`, evaluate if `std` or an existing dependency (`crossterm`, `serde`, `clap`, `cpal`) already provides the capability.

## 2. Zero Panics in Production Paths
- Production code must never call `unwrap()`, `expect()`, or `panic!()`.
- All fallible operations must return `lavaterm::Result<T>` (`Result<T, LavaError>`).
- Implement descriptive error variants in `src/lib.rs` (`LavaError`) with manual `Display` and `Error` implementations without unnecessary external macro dependencies.

## 3. Formatting & Linting Gates
- Code formatting must strictly adhere to `rustfmt.toml`:
  ```bash
  cargo fmt --check
  ```
- Code must pass `clippy` with zero warnings under `-D warnings`:
  ```bash
  cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings
  ```

## 4. Performance & Memory Primitives
- Framebuffer rendering loops and scalar field potential evaluations are in the hot path. Avoid heap allocations (`Vec::new()`, `clone()`, `String::from`) inside per-frame or per-pixel loops.
- Use pre-allocated contiguous buffers (`Vec<Rgb>`, `BufWriter<Stdout>`) and in-place slices.
- Protect concurrency with lock-free atomic primitives (`AtomicU64` Seqlock, `AtomicBool` CAS) as seen in `src/audio/ring_buffer.rs`.
